{"occurrence_no":"1110518","record_type":"occ","collection_no":"140648","identified_name":"Ludwigia murchisonae","identified_rank":"species","identified_no":"464713","accepted_name":"Ludwigia murchisonae","accepted_rank":"species","accepted_no":"184547","early_interval":"Middle Aalenian","max_ma":174.7,"min_ma":170.9,"reference_no":"45578","lng":"0.367222","lat":"48.347500","cc":"FR","state":"Pays de la Loire","county":"Sarthe","latlng_basis":"based on nearby landmark","latlng_precision":"seconds","geogscale":"small collection","geogcomments":"A plesiosaur specimen was discovered at the end of the 19th century on the commune of Saint Rémy du Val (at this time Saint Rémy du Plain – Sarthe Département) by the geologist Albert GUILLIER (1839–1885). The first mention of this discovery in the Lower Oolite with Terebratula perovalis is found in ‘Géologie du département de la Sarthe’ (GUILLIER 1886), a posthumous work. The local Lower Oolite with Terebratula perovalis level was described as: “Les parties formées de sables siliceux sont assez fréquentes, par exemple, aux environs de Saint Rémy du Plain... In the scientific collection catalogue of Le Mans Museums, the Curator PAUL DELAUNAY briefly and imprecisely described this fossil and its geographical occurrence as: “Rachis de saurien jurassique recueilli à Mamers par GUILLIER” (DELAUNAY 1932: 84). This mention is registered on the label still accompanying the specimen. It should be noted that the city of Mamers is located 8 km East to Saint Rémy du Val. As no other vertebrate skeleton is kept in the collections of Le Mans Museums, it is clear that the geographical indication given by DELAUNAY was an oversimplification and that his specimen corresponds to GUILLER’s specimen (N. MOREL, pers. observ.).","environment":"peritidal","primary_reference":"P. Vincent, N. Bardet, and N. Morel. 2007. An elasmosaurid plesiosaur from the Aalenian (Middle Jurassic) of Western France. Neues Jahrbuch für Geologie und Paläontologie, Abhandlungen 243(3):363-370","phylum":"Mollusca","phylum_no":"7805","class":"Cephalopoda","class_no":"12315","order":"Ammonitida","order_no":"84931","family":"Graphoceratidae","family_no":"257907","genus":"Ludwigia","genus_no":"15136"},
{"occurrence_no":"1110519","record_type":"occ","collection_no":"140648","identified_name":"Elasmosauridae indet.","identified_rank":"family","accepted_name":"Elasmosauridae","accepted_rank":"family","accepted_no":"38175","early_interval":"Middle Aalenian","max_ma":174.7,"min_ma":170.9,"reference_no":"45578","lng":"0.367222","lat":"48.347500","cc":"FR","state":"Pays de la Loire","county":"Sarthe","latlng_basis":"based on nearby landmark","latlng_precision":"seconds","geogscale":"small collection","geogcomments":"A plesiosaur specimen was discovered at the end of the 19th century on the commune of Saint Rémy du Val (at this time Saint Rémy du Plain – Sarthe Département) by the geologist Albert GUILLIER (1839–1885). The first mention of this discovery in the Lower Oolite with Terebratula perovalis is found in ‘Géologie du département de la Sarthe’ (GUILLIER 1886), a posthumous work. The local Lower Oolite with Terebratula perovalis level was described as: “Les parties formées de sables siliceux sont assez fréquentes, par exemple, aux environs de Saint Rémy du Plain... In the scientific collection catalogue of Le Mans Museums, the Curator PAUL DELAUNAY briefly and imprecisely described this fossil and its geographical occurrence as: “Rachis de saurien jurassique recueilli à Mamers par GUILLIER” (DELAUNAY 1932: 84). This mention is registered on the label still accompanying the specimen. It should be noted that the city of Mamers is located 8 km East to Saint Rémy du Val. As no other vertebrate skeleton is kept in the collections of Le Mans Museums, it is clear that the geographical indication given by DELAUNAY was an oversimplification and that his specimen corresponds to GUILLER’s specimen (N. MOREL, pers. observ.).","environment":"peritidal","primary_reference":"P. Vincent, N. Bardet, and N. Morel. 2007. An elasmosaurid plesiosaur from the Aalenian (Middle Jurassic) of Western France. Neues Jahrbuch für Geologie und Paläontologie, Abhandlungen 243(3):363-370","phylum":"Chordata","phylum_no":"33815","class":"Reptilia","class_no":"36322","order":"Plesiosauria","order_no":"36454","family":"Elasmosauridae","family_no":"38175"},
